  "response": "Where both the accused's act and a later act of a third party are advanced at trial as the explanation of a death, the court must first settle who answers the causal question and by what test. Causation in a homicide trial is determined by the judge alone as a pure question of law; that cannot be squared with the Court of Appeal's holding that causation was a question of fact properly left to the jury, since the allocation of the issue to the tribunal of law and its allocation to the tribunal of fact cannot both be true of one and the same trial.\n\nThe inquiry itself proceeds in two stages. Factual causation is established by the \"but for\" test, and as a result satisfaction of that test is sufficient to establish legal causation as well. The notion that satisfaction of the \"but for\" test suffices for legal causation cannot be reconciled with the settled requirement that legal causation requires the defendant's conduct to be an operating and substantial cause of the death; a bare condition of the outcome and an operating and substantial cause are not the same thing, and the two positions cannot stand together.\n\nThe authorities on intervening medical treatment repay close attention. R v Jordan treated palpably wrong medical treatment as breaking the chain of causation. The reach of liability where doctors have been at fault is a recurring question; any negligent medical treatment administered after a wounding relieves the defendant of liability for the resulting death. By contrast, R v Smith held that negligent medical treatment did not break the chain of causation from a bayonet wound. That holding rules out any principle under which negligent treatment following a wounding would of itself relieve the defendant of liability for the ensuing death, and it shows that the operating-and-substantial-cause requirement identified above is the governing criterion.\n\nIn the case under consideration, at least one of two things was true when the victim reached hospital, and perhaps both: the wound inflicted by the defendant was still haemorrhaging, or the hospital administered a drug to which the victim had a recorded allergy. The condition of the wound on admission is the evidence from which the causal finding on these facts is drawn. The defendant's wound was an operating and substantial cause of death at the moment of death, which confirms the trial court's conclusion that the hospital's treatment did not break the chain of causation from the defendant's attack.\n\nThe question ultimately resolves into a single binary choice. Either the chain of causation between the defendant's act and the victim's death was broken by the intervening act, or the defendant's act remained an operating cause of the victim's death at the time the victim died: one of the two must be so, and they cannot both be so. The court's task is to decide which, by asking whether the original wound retained its potency at the moment of death or whether the later act had supplanted it altogether.",
